==================================================
Lab 4: Server-Side Template Injection in an Unknown Language with a Documented Exploit
==================================================

=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT
=======================

A shopping website shows a message on the home page through the message URL parameter.
The server renders that value with a template engine, but the engine is not named on the page.
We identify it from its error messages, then reuse a published exploit for that engine.

Goal: run the command that deletes /home/carlos/morale.txt.

==============================
HOW THE WEBSITE NORMALLY WORKS
==============================

When the page loads with message=<text>, the server renders the text into the home page.
A normal message, such as "Unfortunately this product is out of stock", is shown as plain text.

=======================
 WHAT IS THE PROBLEM
=======================

The message value is placed into the template before it is evaluated.
Template syntax in the value is therefore processed by the engine, not displayed as text.

===========================
THREE THINGS TO UNDERSTAND
===========================

Fuzzing: Sending a string with many template delimiters, such as ${{<%[%'"}}%\`. Each engine reacts differently, and the error message reveals which one is in use.
Handlebars: A JavaScript template engine. Its expressions use double curly braces, and block helpers such as #with and #each can reach JavaScript objects.
Prototype chain: JavaScript objects inherit properties. The constructor property leads to the Function constructor, which can turn a string into executable code.

======================================
THE ATTACK
======================================

First, we send the fuzz string. The error message identifies Handlebars.

Then we send the published exploit, modified to run our command. The nested #with and #each blocks build a string
that reaches the Function constructor through lookup "constructor". The code it runs is:

return require('child_process').exec('rm /home/carlos/morale.txt');

The text "wrtz" at the start is plain literal text, not a template expression. It is part of the published exploit's format.
The server runs the code, and the rm command deletes Carlos's file. The page output does not matter.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY
============================

Open the lab and submit the fuzz string in the message parameter. Note the error and identify Handlebars.
Search for the Handlebars exploit, modify it to call child_process.exec with the rm command, URL-encode it, and load it as the message parameter.
The lab is marked as solved once the URL loads.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES
=========================

* It asks for the lab's base URL.
* It stores the exploit as a triple-quoted string, so the template's line breaks and indentation are kept.
* It sends the exploit through params=, so requests encodes it once. Pre-encoding would double-encode it.
* It prints the status code. The output is not needed, since the lab checks the file deletion.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS
=============================

The engine evaluates user input as template code. Handlebars can reach JavaScript's built-in objects through their properties,
so an attacker can climb from a string to the Function constructor and run arbitrary JavaScript on the server.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS
=============================

Never pass user input into a template as template source. Pass it in as data, for example through a variable.
Keep user input out of anything the engine treats as code, including values taken from URL parameters.
Restrict the runtime so it cannot call child_process or other system modules.

=============================
 KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
=============================
A fuzz string reveals the template engine from its error messages.
A published exploit can be reused once the engine is identified, but it must be read and adapted to the lab.
Never let user input reach template source, even in a message parameter.
